{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And you shall pursue your enemies.</b> It is difficult: after He promised peace and security, what place is there to say so? Indeed, it intends that what He promised concerns what the nations might do — that they would not do evil nor cause destruction — but not what Israel might do to the nations. And this is the essence of the miracle of the promise: lest they say that the peace and the promise means that they too — Israel — would not do evil nor cause destruction to the nations, and so they would be found acting reciprocally — Scripture comes to say <b>and you shall pursue</b> etc., for you shall pursue them and destroy them, and even so you shall dwell in security and in peace, and no sword shall pass through your land. And the reason Scripture calls them enemies is not because they come to besiege the Holy City, for if so there would be no security and no peace for its inhabitants; rather, it calls them enemies because they are enemies of HaShem — the wicked of the nations are called enemies of HaShem and our enemies — and also because HaShem knows that all the nations are, by their very nature, haters of Israel, and this is a foundational hatred that has no exchange." "<b>And they shall fall</b> etc. It says <b>by the sword</b> — meaning, the falling of the enemies before the people of HaShem will be in the measure of a falling that comes from the sword. And by way of remez, it intends, along the lines of what they said regarding the miracle of the Hasmoneans (Josephus 22), that an angel was seen drawing a sword against the enemy — and this is what He alluded to by saying <b>and they shall fall</b> etc. <b>by the sword</b> — meaning, the one drawn by the angel."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:42.676273310Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}